everyone knows Sony's PlayStation Vita  their powerful little handheld device  well as of last week in North America  everyone can now buy a PlayStation TV  which is essentially a PlayStation Vita  in this compact format without a screen  or controls essentially you hook it up  to your television and you can sync it  up with a DualShock 3 or a Dualshock 4  play video games PSP games PSone  classics but you can also stream video  from your Playstation 4 in one room to a  Playstation TV in another room now those  are a lot of promising features but the  real question is how well does it work  it turns out nearly every aspect of the  PlayStation TV has limitations that may  catch you off guard it's not that the  PlayStation TV doesn't do it it claims  but it doesn't do it as well as you  expect first and foremost the  PlayStation TV is ostensibly a vita  that's traded in its battery display  touch capabilities and controls for HDMI  and wireless controller connectivity it  still utilizes Vita cartridges and  memory cards and thankfully existing  Vita owners can move their memory card  to the PlayStation TV and retain all of  their installed apps and games it  outputs video over HDMI at 480p 720p and  1080i and if you crave faster than  wireless Internet connectivity  there's an Ethernet port at the ready  when it comes to game support there's a  decent selection of Vita games that work  flawlessly but obviously without support  for true touch-based controls there are  limitations it's not all bad  Sony implemented a fair solution for  dealing with slower paced games that use  touch controls from menus and things  like that when playing video games with  either of the supported controllers be  it a dual shock 3 or 4 l3 or r3 will  activate a cursor that can be controlled  with the corresponding analog stick and  shoulder buttons the system works as  intended but it's cumbersome at best and  alas the DualShock 4's touchpad is left  unused of course not every Vita game  relies on touch controls so it's not  totally damming that the PS TV isn't  mature enough to account for those  titles there are currently over 140 Vita  games that are supported  not counting PSP PSone classics  PlayStation minis and PlayStation mobile  games if you count those libraries then  the number adds up to roughly 7 played  games currently compatibility is  dictated by a whitelist that's defined  by Sony which means that future titles  retail or downloadable may not be  supported without  firmware update also just because a game  doesn't rely on touch controls such as  Metal Gear Solid HD collection there's  still a chance that it won't work on the  PlayStation TV given the discrepancy in  controls is understandable why not all  Vita games might work but it's not  immediately clear why there isn't  support for the entire library of  downloadable PSone classics and PSP  games both of these supported  controllers have the necessary inputs  some games that work in the Vita like  the PSP game Castlevania the Dracula X  Chronicles just aren't supported  hopefully Sony can patch these gaps in  support in the near future when it comes  to support for non gaming apps there's  one app that isn't supported by the  PlayStation TV which absolutely should  be netflix even if you've never use the  app on your Vita if you have a  PlayStation TV connected to your monitor  in lieu of a console streaming video  would be a great pillar of support for  this little device your only video  options at the moment include  crunchyroll crackle and sony's video  unlimited service if you're transferring  an existing Vita content to the  PlayStation TV then you're more than  likely to find games that don't work if  however you're starting from scratch  sony has built a special version of the  PlayStation Network store for  PlayStation TV that only displays  supported content using remote play with  the PlayStation 4 is a big selling point  for the PlayStation TV that will no  doubt entice a lot of PlayStation 4  owners the promise of being able to play  games in one room while your PlayStation  4 is and another sounds great but for  one reason or another be it sony's video  compression methodology or network  limitations there's always a noticeable  amount of latency to contend with  it doesn't matter if you're  communicating over a wireless network or  plugged right into the router remote  play works but there's noticeable lag  and latency issues that will make it  unfeasible to play competitive games at  a competitive level unfortunately we can  only recommend remote play via the  PlayStation TV for slower paced games  that don't require twitch precision  the PlayStation TV has a lot of  potential but it's obvious that Sony  still has a lot of work to do remote  play is only viable for some types of  games and there are plenty of games that  should work from the Vita PSP and ps1  libraries that are inexplicably  unsupported at least the games that are  natively supported do play really well  with the exception of some video games  that use the PlayStation TV's emulated  touch controls which are pretty  cumbersome in practice lastly so needs  to make sure that video apps such as  Netflix actually work because there's no  technical reason why they shouldn't at  this point it feels like the PlayStation  TV came out too soon and its software  could have benefited from a few extra  months of development
 

